</LocalTags>
<Routines>
<Routine Name="Logic" Type="RLL">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Collect controller CPU information; poll for CPU, Trend, Memory and Connection data if Enabled.]]>
</Description>
<RLLContent>
<Labels>
<Label Name="End"/>
<Label Name="SkipText"/>
</Labels>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Copyright © Rockwell Automation, Inc.  All Rights Reserved.
============================================================
L_CPU_18:  LOGIX -- Processor Utilization (Major Rev. 18)
============================================================
Revision 3.1-01 Release  2014-09-03:  See V3.1-xx Release Notes for details.
V1.x and V2.x Revision History archived.
V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Added Inf_Lib. Removed "Main Task" scan time information,
as this is now covered by L_TaskMon instances. Changed
Operator Commands (OCmds) to Maintenance Commands (MCmds).
V3.1-00  2014-06-30:  Unmodified for Logix Diagnostics Library V3.1, up-rev only.
V3.1-01  2014-09-03:  Unmodified, up-rev only for V3.1-01 public release.
============================================================
This Instruction monitors the Logix controller executing it.  It provides
several diagnostics that may be usefull in troubleshooting system
performance issues.  This information is good to have handy on-screen
when calling Rockwell Automation Technical Support for assistance.  Let
your Tech Support engineer know you have this info when you call.

Information includes:
*  Controller Identity information (catalog number, firmware revision)
*  Communications Timeslice setting
*  Minor Fault information
*  Memory Usage/Free statistics
*  CPU Utilization for various controller and user Tasks
*  Communications data including CPU available for and used by comms,
Trend object usage and Connections used for all communications
============================================================
SHOW LOCAL STRINGS
============================================================
The STRINGs containing the text associated with each instance of this
instruction cannot be Input or Output Parameters, because STRINGs are not
 'atomic' types. (Inputs and Outputs must be SINT, INT, DINT, REAL, or BOOL.)

In order to make it easier to find and configure these STRINGs, this rung
was added.  THE JMP (and the LBL on the following rung)
MUST NOT BE REMOVED!!!

To view the STRINGs, go to the instruction instance (in LD or FBD), RIGHT-click
to bring up the context menu and select "Open Instruction Logic".
This rung will appear with the STRING values for the selected instance.
You may double-click the STRING values here to modify them as well.

#####  V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Added Inf_Lib  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JMP(SkipText)CONCAT(Cfg_Desc,Cfg_Label,Cfg_Tag)CONCAT(Cfg_SupportInfo,Inf_Lib,Inf_Type);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
HANDLE COMMANDS
============================================================
These rungs handle Commands received by this instruction.

If Maintenance sends the Enable Command, enable data collection.

#####  V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Operator Command changed to Maintenance Command.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(SkipText)XIC(MCmd_Enable)OTU(MCmd_Enable)OTL(Wrk_Enabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If Maintenance sends the Disable Command, turn off data collection.

#####  V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Operator Command changed to Maintenance Command.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(MCmd_Disable)OTU(MCmd_Disable)OTU(Wrk_Enabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
HANDLE CONFIGURATION
============================================================
These rungs handle configuration data for this instruction

Cfg_WindowT:  The Window Time (usually 1.0 sec.) is the time over which 
Controller Task Times (in percent) are calculated]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MUL(Cfg_WindowT,1000000.0,Ref_WindowT)LES(Ref_WindowT,0)MOV(1.0,Cfg_WindowT)MOV(1000000,Ref_WindowT);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Cfg_UpdateT: The Update Time throttles the collection of data.  Outputs
of this instruction are gathered at this interval.  This should be at least as
long as the Window Time (Cfg_WindowT), ideally a multiple of it.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MUL(Cfg_UpdateT,1000,Wrk_UpdateT.PRE)XIC(Wrk_UpdateT.PRE.31)MOV(5,Cfg_UpdateT)MOV(5000,Wrk_UpdateT.PRE);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Slot number is a USINT (valid values = 0 to 255, althoug
you'll probably never see one higher than 16.....).
If the slot number is not valid, clear it to a valid value.

Unfortunately we can't just GSV it out of the controller.......]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LIM(256,Cfg_SlotNumber,-1)CLR(Cfg_SlotNumber);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the user changes the Window time, set up the controller path
and send (MSG) it to the controller's TaskMetrics object.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NEQ(Wrk_WindowT,Wrk_PrevWindowT)[MOV(2,Ref_MsgSetWindow.Path.LEN) ,MOV(1,Ref_MsgSetWindow.Path.DATA[0]) ,MOV(Cfg_SlotNumber,Ref_MsgSetWindow.Path.DATA[1]) ,MSG(Ref_MsgSetWindow) ,XIC(Ref_MsgSetWindow.EN) XIC(Ref_MsgSetWindow.DN) MOV(Wrk_WindowT,Wrk_PrevWindowT) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The Continuous Task Instance number (Task instance 1 to 31,
or zero if NO Continuous Task) is used as a pointer in logic below,
so we need to make sure it is valid so as not to fault the controller.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LIM(32,Cfg_ContTaskInstance,-1)CLR(Cfg_ContTaskInstance);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
PROCESSOR UTILIZATION DATA UPDATE TIMER
============================================================
This rung is a self repeating timer for triggering data collection at the Update Time interval.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Wrk_UpdateT.DN)TON(Wrk_UpdateT,?,?);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
EVERY UPDATE
============================================================
:  Gat Controller Identity Data

Even though it won't be changing, every update we gather the controller Device Identity data.
Place the Device Identity datat appropriately in the Output (Identity) registers.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Wrk_UpdateT.DN)[FLL(0,Ref_Out.Identity.Name[0],33) ,GSV(ControllerDevice,,DeviceName,Ref_Out.Identity.Name[0]) ,MOV(1,Ref_Out.Identity.VendorID) MOV(14,Ref_Out.Identity.ProductType) GSV(ControllerDevice,,ProductCode,Ref_Out.Identity.ProductCode) ,GSV(ControllerDevice,,ProductRev,Wrk_IdentityProductRev) BTD(Wrk_IdentityProductRev,0,Ref_Out.Identity.Major,0,8) BTD(Wrk_IdentityProductRev,8,Ref_Out.Identity.Minor,0,8) ,GSV(ControllerDevice,,SerialNumber,Ref_Out.Identity.Serial) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Check Controller Firmware version and Controller Product Code

If the Major Revision of the firmware is not 18, or if the
controller Product Code is for a SOFT controller (SoftLogix, Emulator, etc.),
then the results of this instruction will not be reliable.  Set the
"Wrong Version" status in this case.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[NEQ(Ref_Out.Identity.Major,18) ,EQU(Ref_Out.Identity.ProductCode,15) ,EQU(Ref_Out.Identity.ProductCode,53) ,EQU(Ref_Out.Identity.ProductCode,62) ,EQU(Ref_Out.Identity.ProductCode,63) ]OTE(Sts_WrongVersion);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Controller Time Slice and Minor Faults

Then get the Controller Time Slice configuration and the Minor Fault
information and format that into the Output data.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Wrk_UpdateT.DN) [GSV(ControllerDevice,,Status,Ref_Out.Identity.Status) ,GSV(Controller,,TimeSlice,Ref_Out.TimeSlice) ,BTD(0,0,Ref_Out.MinorFaultCount,16,16) GSV(FaultLog,,MinorEvents,Ref_Out.MinorFaultCount) ,GSV(FaultLog,,MinorFaultBits,Wrk_MinorFaultBits) ,GSV(Module,,LEDStatus,Ref_Out.IOLEDStatus) ,GSV(Module,,ForceStatus,Ref_Out.ForceStatus) ] ,XIC(Wrk_MinorFaultBits.4) OTE(Ref_Out.MinorFaultProg) ,XIC(Wrk_MinorFaultBits.6) OTE(Ref_Out.MinorFaultTask) ,XIC(Wrk_MinorFaultBits.9) OTE(Ref_Out.MinorFaultPort) ,XIC(Wrk_MinorFaultBits.10) OTE(Ref_Out.MinorFaultBatt) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="12" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If this instruction is NOT ENABLED for data collection,
clear the Trend Object Usage High Water value,
reset the update timer, and clear the various status bits.

Then skip the remainder of the code and jump to the end to reduce scan impact.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Sts_Enabled)[CLR(Ref_Out.CommUse.TrendHighWater) ,OTU(Wrk_Poll) OTU(Sts_Done) OTU(Sts_MsgErr) JMP(End) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="13" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
MESSAGES TO CIP OBJECTS IN CONTROLLER
============================================================
Much of the information we need can only be obtianed via CIP GENERIC Messages.

Each update, we set a latch that is held ON undil ALL the messages
to the controller to get performance data have finished or errored.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Wrk_UpdateT.DN)OTL(Wrk_Poll)OTU(Sts_Done)OTU(Sts_MsgErr);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="14" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Trend Object Usage

Trend objects are used by Trends (from RSLogix 5000), but are primarily
used by RSLinx and RSLinx Enterprise to group data for regular reading by the HMI.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Wrk_Poll)[MOV(2,Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.Path.LEN) ,MOV(1,Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.Path.DATA[0]) ,MOV(Cfg_SlotNumber,Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.Path.DATA[1]) ,MSG(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="15" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Once the Trend Object Usage Message completes,
store the data in the Output.

Note: the response includes the number of attributes and the attribute numbers,
so the data values are offset in the array.  See the L_CPU document for more information.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.EN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.DN)[COP(Ref_TrendObjData[3],Ref_Out.CommUse.MaxTrendInstance,1) COP(Ref_TrendObjData[7],Ref_Out.CommUse.NumTrendInstances,1) ,GRT(Ref_Out.CommUse.NumTrendInstances,Ref_Out.CommUse.TrendHighWater) MOV(Ref_Out.CommUse.NumTrendInstances,Ref_Out.CommUse.TrendHighWater) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="16" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Memory Usage

Once the Task 1 Scan Time Message completes (or fails),
go get the User Memory Usage data.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.EN)[XIC(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.DN) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.ER) ][MOV(2,Ref_MsgGetMemUse.Path.LEN) ,MOV(1,Ref_MsgGetMemUse.Path.DATA[0]) ,MOV(Cfg_SlotNumber,Ref_MsgGetMemUse.Path.DATA[1]) ,MSG(Ref_MsgGetMemUse) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="17" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Once the User Memory Usage Message completes,
store the data in the Output.

Note: the response includes the number of attributes and the attribute numbers,
so the data values are offset in the array.  See the L_CPU document for more information.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Ref_MsgGetMemUse.EN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetMemUse.DN)[COP(Ref_MemUseData[4],Wrk_MemUseFreeIO,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseFreeIO,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.FreeIO) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[6],Wrk_MemUseFreeDataLogic,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseFreeDataLogic,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.FreeDataLogic) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[8],Wrk_MemUseFreeGen,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseFreeGen,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.FreeGen) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[10],Wrk_MemUseFreeSafety,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseFreeSafety,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.FreeSafety) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[15],Wrk_MemUseTotalIO,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseTotalIO,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.TotalIO) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[17],Wrk_MemUseTotalDataLogic,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseTotalDataLogic,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.TotalDataLogic) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[19],Wrk_MemUseTotalGen,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseTotalGen,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.TotalGen) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[21],Wrk_MemUseTotalSafety,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseTotalSafety,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.TotalSafety) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[26],Wrk_MemUseMinIO,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMinIO,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MinIO) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[28],Wrk_MemUseMinDataLogic,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMinDataLogic,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MinDataLogic) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[30],Wrk_MemUseMinGen,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMinGen,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MinGen) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[32],Wrk_MemUseMinSafety,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMinSafety,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MinSafety) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[37],Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockIO,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockIO,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MaxBlockIO) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[39],Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockDataLogic,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockDataLogic,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MaxBlockDataLogic) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[41],Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockGen,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockGen,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MaxBlockGen) ,COP(Ref_MemUseData[43],Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockSafety,1) MUL(Wrk_MemUseMaxBlockSafety,4,Ref_Out.MemUse.MaxBlockSafety) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="18" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Connection Usage

Once the Memory Usage Message completes, go get the Connection Use data.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Ref_MsgGetMemUse.EN)[XIC(Ref_MsgGetMemUse.DN) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetMemUse.ER) ][MOV(2,Ref_MsgGetConnUse.Path.LEN) ,MOV(1,Ref_MsgGetConnUse.Path.DATA[0]) ,MOV(Cfg_SlotNumber,Ref_MsgGetConnUse.Path.DATA[1]) ,MSG(Ref_MsgGetConnUse) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="19" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Once the Connection Usage Message completes,
store the data in the Output.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.EN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.DN)[MOV(Ref_ConnData[3],Ref_Out.ConnUse.TotalAllow) MOV(Ref_ConnData[6],Ref_Out.ConnUse.TotalCurr) MOV(Ref_ConnData[9],Ref_Out.ConnUse.TotalHiWater) ,MOV(Ref_ConnData[12],Ref_Out.ConnUse.IOAllow) MOV(Ref_ConnData[15],Ref_Out.ConnUse.IOCurr) MOV(Ref_ConnData[18],Ref_Out.ConnUse.IOHiWater) ,MOV(Ref_ConnData[21],Ref_Out.ConnUse.ProdAllow) MOV(Ref_ConnData[24],Ref_Out.ConnUse.ProdCurr) MOV(Ref_ConnData[27],Ref_Out.ConnUse.ProdHiWater) ,MOV(Ref_ConnData[30],Ref_Out.ConnUse.ConsAllow) MOV(Ref_ConnData[33],Ref_Out.ConnUse.ConsCurr) MOV(Ref_ConnData[36],Ref_Out.ConnUse.ConsHiWater) ,MOV(Ref_ConnData[39],Ref_Out.ConnUse.MSGCurr) MOV(Ref_ConnData[42],Ref_Out.ConnUse.MSGHiWater) ,MOV(Ref_ConnData[45],Ref_Out.ConnUse.IncomCurr) MOV(Ref_ConnData[48],Ref_Out.ConnUse.IncomHiWater) ,COP(Ref_ConnData[51],Ref_Out.ConnUse.UCBsAllow,1) COP(Ref_ConnData[59],Ref_Out.ConnUse.UCBsCurr,1) COP(Ref_ConnData[55],Ref_Out.ConnUse.UCBsHiWater,1) ,MOV(Ref_ConnData[63],Ref_Out.ConnUse.MCacheAllow) MOV(Ref_ConnData[66],Ref_Out.ConnUse.MCacheCurr) MOV(Ref_ConnData[69],Ref_Out.ConnUse.MCacheHiWater) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="20" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Task Times (CPU Utilization)

NOTE:  The Tast Times data are version specific.  Don't even try to process
these items if we're not Version 18 or if we're a Soft / Emulated controller!

Once the Connection Usage Message completes (or fails),
go get the Operating System Task Times data.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Sts_WrongVersion)XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.EN)[XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.DN) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.ER) ][MOV(2,Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.Path.LEN) ,MOV(1,Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.Path.DATA[0]) ,MOV(Cfg_SlotNumber,Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.Path.DATA[1]) ,MSG(Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="21" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[And once the Operating System Task Times Message completes (don't bother if it fails!),
go get the User Task Times data.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Sts_WrongVersion)XIC(Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.EN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.DN)[MOV(2,Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.Path.LEN) ,MOV(1,Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.Path.DATA[0]) ,MOV(Cfg_SlotNumber,Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.Path.DATA[1]) ,MSG(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="22" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Once BOTH TaskTime Messages complete,
store the data in the Output.

Note: the response format is Logix version 18.xx specific]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Sts_WrongVersion)XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.EN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.DN)GRT(Ref_TaskTimeData[0],0)[CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.Comms,100.0*(Ref_TaskTimeData[2]+Ref_TaskTimeData[3]+Ref_TaskTimeData[4]+Ref_TaskTimeData[20]+Ref_TaskTimeData[21]+Ref_TaskTimeData[23]+Ref_TaskTimeData[29]+Ref_TaskTimeData[42]+Ref_TaskTimeData[43]+Ref_TaskTimeData[44]+Ref_TaskTimeData[45]+Ref_TaskTimeData[46]+Ref_TaskTimeData[47]+Ref_TaskTimeData[48]+Ref_TaskTimeData[49]+Ref_TaskTimeData[50]+Ref_TaskTimeData[51])/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.Messaging,100.0*Ref_TaskTimeData[14]/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.Motion,100.0*Ref_TaskTimeData[17]/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.Redundancy,100.0*(Ref_TaskTimeData[30]+Ref_TaskTimeData[31]+Ref_TaskTimeData[32]+Ref_TaskTimeData[34]+Ref_TaskTimeData[41]+Ref_TaskTimeData[52])/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.Safety,100.0*(Ref_TaskTimeData[37]+Ref_TaskTimeData[38]+Ref_TaskTimeData[39])/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.System,100.0*(Ref_TaskTimeData[5]+Ref_TaskTimeData[6]+Ref_TaskTimeData[8]+Ref_TaskTimeData[9]+Ref_TaskTimeData[11]+Ref_TaskTimeData[12]+Ref_TaskTimeData[13]+Ref_TaskTimeData[16]+Ref_TaskTimeData[19]+Ref_TaskTimeData[22]+Ref_TaskTimeData[24]+Ref_TaskTimeData[25]+Ref_TaskTimeData[26]+Ref_TaskTimeData[27]+Ref_TaskTimeData[28]+Ref_TaskTimeData[33]+Ref_TaskTimeData[35]+Ref_TaskTimeData[36]+Ref_TaskTimeData[40]+Ref_TaskTimeData[59]+Ref_TaskTimeData[60]+Ref_TaskTimeData[61]+Ref_TaskTimeData[62]+Ref_TaskTimeData[63])/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="23" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Once BOTH TaskTime Messages complete,
store the data in the Output.

Note: the response format is Logix version 18.xx specific.

#####  V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Modified rung to use configured Continuous Task instance number  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Sts_WrongVersion)XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.EN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.DN)GRT(Ref_TaskTimeData[0],0)[COP(Ref_TaskTimeData[70],Wrk_UserTaskTimes[0],32) ,CLR(Wrk_TaskTimesSum) ,FAL(Wrk_TaskTimesSumCtrl,?,?,all,Wrk_TaskTimesSum,Wrk_TaskTimesSum+Wrk_UserTaskTimes[Wrk_TaskTimesSumCtrl.POS]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Ref_TaskTimeData[0]-Ref_TaskTimeData[6]-Ref_TaskTimeData[8]-Ref_TaskTimeData[9]-Ref_TaskTimeData[10]-Ref_TaskTimeData[12]-Ref_TaskTimeData[16]-Ref_TaskTimeData[17]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Ref_TaskTimeData[19]-Ref_TaskTimeData[21]-Ref_TaskTimeData[22]-Ref_TaskTimeData[23]-Ref_TaskTimeData[24]-Ref_TaskTimeData[25]-Ref_TaskTimeData[26]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Ref_TaskTimeData[27]-Ref_TaskTimeData[28]-Ref_TaskTimeData[29]-Ref_TaskTimeData[30]-Ref_TaskTimeData[31]-Ref_TaskTimeData[33]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Ref_TaskTimeData[34]-Ref_TaskTimeData[35]-Ref_TaskTimeData[38]-Ref_TaskTimeData[39]-Ref_TaskTimeData[40]-Ref_TaskTimeData[3]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Ref_TaskTimeData[2]-Ref_TaskTimeData[41]-Ref_TaskTimeData[52]-Ref_TaskTimeData[60]-Ref_TaskTimeData[61]-Ref_TaskTimeData[62]-Ref_TaskTimeData[63]) ,EQU(Cfg_ContTaskInstance,0) [CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.PeriodicAndEvent,100.0*(Wrk_TaskTimesSum+Ref_TaskTimeData[10])/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.ContinuousOrFree,100.0*Ref_TaskTimeData[1]/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Wrk_TaskTimesSum) ] ,NEQ(Cfg_ContTaskInstance,0) [CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.PeriodicAndEvent,100.0*(Wrk_TaskTimesSum+Ref_TaskTimeData[10]-Wrk_UserTaskTimes[Cfg_ContTaskInstance])/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CPUUse.ContinuousOrFree,100.0*Wrk_UserTaskTimes[Cfg_ContTaskInstance]/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Wrk_TaskTimesSum+Wrk_UserTaskTimes[Cfg_ContTaskInstance]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,0.01*Ref_Out.TimeSlice*Wrk_CommsSubtotal) ] ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Ref_TaskTimeData[5]-Ref_TaskTimeData[13]-Ref_TaskTimeData[14]-Ref_TaskTimeData[20]-Ref_TaskTimeData[32]-Ref_TaskTimeData[36]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Ref_TaskTimeData[37]-Ref_TaskTimeData[42]-Ref_TaskTimeData[43]-Ref_TaskTimeData[44]-Ref_TaskTimeData[45]-Ref_TaskTimeData[46]) ,CPT(Wrk_CommsSubtotal,Wrk_CommsSubtotal-Ref_TaskTimeData[47]-Ref_TaskTimeData[48]-Ref_TaskTimeData[49]-Ref_TaskTimeData[50]-Ref_TaskTimeData[51]-Ref_TaskTimeData[59]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CommUse.RespCPUAvail,100.0*Wrk_CommsSubtotal/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ,CPT(Ref_Out.CommUse.RespCPUAct,100.0*Ref_TaskTimeData[4]/Ref_TaskTimeData[0]) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="24" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If all the Messages we're using went DONE, clear the Message Error Status.

#####  V3.0.00  2013-06-30:  Modified to remove Main Task Scan Time message.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Ref_MsgSetWindow.DN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.DN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetMemUse.DN)XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.DN)[XIC(Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.DN) XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.DN) ,XIC(Sts_WrongVersion) ]OTU(Sts_MsgErr);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="25" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If any of the Messages we're using ERRORED, set the Message Error Status.

#####  V3.0.00  2013-06-30:  Modified to remove Main Task Scan Time message.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Ref_MsgSetWindow.ER) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetTrendObjUse.ER) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetMemUse.ER) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.ER) ,XIO(Sts_WrongVersion) [XIC(Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.ER) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.ER) ] ]OTL(Sts_MsgErr);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="26" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If this is the correct version of firmware and a supported controller,
once the Task Times are done (or errored), we're dont with this poll.

If this is NOT the correct verion or a supported controller,
we skipped the Task Times.  We're done when the Connection Usage
has finished (or errored).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Sts_WrongVersion) [XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.EN) [XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.DN) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetUserTaskTimes.ER) ] ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.EN) XIC(Ref_MsgGetOSTaskTimes.ER) ] ,XIC(Sts_WrongVersion) XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.EN) [XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.DN) ,XIC(Ref_MsgGetConnUse.ER) ] ][OTU(Wrk_Poll) ,XIO(Sts_MsgErr) OTL(Sts_Done) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="27" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
STATUS AND READIES
============================================================
If the instruction is Enabled:

* Show the Enabled status.
* If it is in the midst of polling the CPU data, show the Busy status.
*  If the Update timer is timing but the poll is NOT busy, show the Timing status.
(Sts_MsgErr and Sts_Done are handled above.)
*  Show Ready to receive the Disable command.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(End)XIC(Wrk_Enabled)[OTE(Sts_Enabled) ,XIC(Wrk_Poll) OTE(Sts_Busy) ,XIC(Wrk_UpdateT.TT) XIO(Wrk_Poll) OTE(Sts_Timing) ,OTE(Rdy_Disable) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="28" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Finally, if the instruction is NOT Enabled, it is Ready to receive the Enable command.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Wrk_Enabled)OTE(Rdy_Enable);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="Prescan" Type="RLL">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Initialize "high water" data, "previous" data. Stop active polling and set to Disabled state.]]>
</Description>
<RLLContent>
<Labels/>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
PRESCAN :  Initialize
============================================================
This rung clears the "previous" Window Time configuration, forcing the
instruction to write the window time MSG when first scanned, and
clears the instruction-trackedTrend use  "high water" value.

Any active polling is stopped and the instruction is set to the
"Disabled" state.  Data collection must be manually Enabled.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[CLR(Wrk_PrevWindowT)CLR(Ref_Out.CommUse.TrendHighWater)OTU(Wrk_Poll)OTU(Wrk_Enabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA["Lurking" Operator Commands are cleared.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTU(MCmd_Disable)OTU(MCmd_Enable);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
</Routines>
</AddOnInstructionDefinition>
</AddOnInstructionDefinitions>
<Tags Use="Context">
<Tag Name="L_CPU_TrendObjAttrList" TagType="Base" DataType="INT" Dimensions="5" Radix="Decimal" Constant="true" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[List of Attributes for GetAttrList on Trend Object]]>
</Description>
<Data>02 00 02 00 03 00 00 00 00 00</Data>
<Data Format="Decorated">
<Array DataType="INT" Dimensions="5" Radix="Decimal">
<Element Index="[0]" Value="2"/>
<Element Index="[1]" Value="2"/>
<Element Index="[2]" Value="3"/>
<Element Index="[3]" Value="0"/>
<Element Index="[4]" Value="0"/>
</Array>
</Data>
</Tag>
<Tag Name="L_CPU_MsgGetOSTaskTimes" TagType="Base" DataType="MESSAGE" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Message: Get Controller OS Task Times]]>
</Description>
<Data Format="Message">
<MessageParameters MessageType="CIP Generic" RequestedLength="2" ConnectedFlag="2" ConnectionPath="EtherNet_IO" CommTypeCode="0" ServiceCode="16#004c" ObjectType="16#0335" TargetObject="1" AttributeNumber="16#0000" LocalIndex="0" LocalElement="L_CPU_TaskTimeListOSStart"
 DestinationTag="L_CPU_TaskTimeData[0]"/>
</Data>
</Tag>
<Tag Name="L_CPU_TaskTimeData" TagType="Base" DataType="DINT" Dimensions="131" Radix="Decimal" Constant="false" ExternalAccess="None">
<Description>
<![CDATA[TaskMetrics Object response to Svc 0x4C]]>
</Description>
<Data>00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 
